:: day_7_1

<<nobr>>
<<removeobjective "Explore the ruins with Hadden">>
<<set $day = 7>>
<<set $mcRegion   = "ashenmoor">>
<<set $mcLocation = "ruins_site">>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I woke to the gray.

Not the gray of stone or steel or the overcast skies that had followed us since Valdmere, this was the particular gray of predawn, the color the world wore before it decided whether the day would be kind or cruel. The color of potential. The color of the hour when the body was most honest, when the mind hadn't yet marshaled its defenses and the flesh beneath the blanket existed in its pure, unnarrated state.

I lay still. The bedroll was warm, my warmth, accumulated through the night, trapped in the canvas like a confession in a sealed letter. The air above the blanket was cool, the kind of mountain-approaching cool that carried the scent of pine resin and moss and the distant mineral tang of elevation. We'd gained three hundred feet yesterday on the road toward the crossroads, and the air had noticed.

The compound hummed.

It was always there now, that low, persistent vibration beneath the skin, the background frequency of transformation. Four days. Four mornings of waking to a body that had been revised overnight, each revision more assertive than the last, each one pushing further past the boundaries of what I could dismiss as minor. The hum had changed character since the first morning. That first morning, it had been a whisper, a suggestion, a preliminary sketch in pencil. Now it was ink. Dark, permanent ink, and the hand holding the pen was steady and the design was becoming legible.

I started with the face. I always started with the face.

My fingertips found my jaw. The right side first, tracing the line from earlobe to chin, the ridge of bone beneath the skin that had once been angular, definitive, the structural announcement of maleness that the world read before it read anything else. The ridge was softer. Not gone, the bone was still there, the mandible still present and accounted for, but the edge had been filed. Rounded. The sharp corner where the jawline met the chin had been eased into a curve, a gentle declination that my fingertips followed with the sick fascination of a man watching his own house being remodeled while he was still inside it.

The left side matched. Symmetrical. The compound was thorough, whatever it did to one side, it mirrored precisely on the other, the bilateral precision of a craftsman who measured twice and cut once and never, ever made mistakes. My jaw was narrower. Not dramatically, not enough to alarm a stranger on the street, but enough that the face I was wearing this morning was measurably different from the face I'd worn a week ago. The bones were being reshaped. Not broken and reset, nothing so crude. The compound worked at the cellular level, directing the osteoclasts and osteoblasts in a coordinated demolition and reconstruction that proceeded millimeter by millimeter, day by day, the old architecture being quietly replaced by new blueprints.

My cheekbones. Higher. More prominent. The soft tissue of the cheeks had thinned, not gaunt, not hollow, but refined, the fat redistributing according to the compound's specifications, leaving the bone more visible, more defined, more, present. I pressed my fingertip against the zygomatic arch and felt the bone respond through less padding than it had yesterday. The cheekbones were emerging. Like mountains being revealed as floodwater receded, the underlying structure surfacing as the masculine soft tissue was stripped away and replaced with something leaner, more sculpted, more deliberately arranged.

My lips. I touched them with my index finger, tracing the outline the way Mira had traced it two nights ago in the firelight. The lower lip was full, genuinely full now, not merely the suggestion of fullness but the real thing, a plush cushion of tissue that my fingertip pressed into and that yielded with a softness that male lips simply didn't possess. The upper lip had changed more dramatically overnight. The cupid's bow, that double-peaked arch that I'd first noticed yesterday as a subtle reshaping, was now a defined feature. A deliberate, sculpted curve at the center of the upper lip that gave the mouth a shape that was, in the gray predawn honesty of fingertip assessment, feminine. Not ambiguously so. Not plausibly masculine from another angle. Feminine. The lips of a woman forming on the face of a man who was running out of days to pretend otherwise.

The color, too. My fingertip came away dry but I could feel the change, the tissue was warmer, the blood closer to the surface, the natural pigmentation deepening from the nondescript tan of male lips to a rosy pink that Mira had noted yesterday and that I could verify now by the simple test of pressing and releasing. The lip blanched under pressure and flushed back to color instantly, healthy, rich, living color, the kind that women achieved with cosmetics and that my body was now producing on its own, the compound converting my mouth into something that advertised itself.

My eyelashes. I blinked deliberately, slowly, feeling the sweep of them against the gray air. Longer. I could see them now, not just at the periphery of vision as I'd reported yesterday, but directly, a dark fringe that entered my visual field with every blink like a curtain briefly drawn and released. The lashes were thick, dark, curved slightly upward in a way that my old lashes, short, straight, invisible, had never been. They framed my vision in a border of femininity that I couldn't remove and couldn't unsee.

I reached higher. My hair. On the pillow, no, on the rolled cloak I was using as a pillow, my hair spread wider than it should have. I gathered it with one hand and held it before my face in the gray light. Longer. Past my ears now, reaching toward my jaw, the growth rate accelerated to something that defied the normal biology of human hair. The texture had changed further, thicker at each strand, but with a suppleness, a weight, a body that my old hair had lacked. And the wave. The hair I'd worn for thirty-two years had been straight, coarse, dark, military-short, obedient. The hair growing from my scalp now had a wave to it, a gentle undulation that gave it movement, that made it respond to gravity with a swing and a bounce that was distractingly alive. I released it. It fell across my forehead and I pushed it back. It fell again. The old hair would have stayed where I put it. This hair had opinions.

Enough with the face. I knew the face. The face was changing and the face would continue to change and cataloging the face was the easy part, the visible part, the part that other people would notice, the part I'd need to manage and conceal and explain away with lies about illness or alchemy or bad luck.

The body was the harder inventory.

I pulled the blanket down to my waist. The cool air touched my chest and the nipples responded immediately, a tightening, a contraction, the swollen nubs pulling inward against the areolae with a sensitivity that was still startling four days in. The response was bilateral and instant, both nipples peaking simultaneously, the tissue behind them registering the temperature change with the amplified reception that the compound had installed.

I looked down.

The pectorals were still there. Still muscled, still defined, thirty-two years of swordwork and hauling and fighting had built a foundation that the compound couldn't dissolve overnight. But the landscape had changed. The chest hair was gone, completely, totally, irreversibly gone. Not a strand. Not a follicle. The skin that stretched over the muscle was smooth, pale, luminous in the gray light with a quality that I could only describe as, radiant. The compound was doing something to the skin itself, not just removing the hair but refining the tissue, increasing the collagen, adjusting the texture until the surface had a softness, a glow, a translucency that was unmistakably feminine.

The nipples. I'd been avoiding the nipples. I looked at them now with the clinical detachment I could manage for approximately three seconds before the clinical detachment collapsed into something rawer.

They were larger. Overnight, again, another increment. The areolae had expanded further, circles of rosy-pink tissue, darker and more vivid than yesterday, each one now wider than the pad of my thumb. The color was striking against the pale skin, defined borders, the pigmentation deepening at the edges where the areola met the surrounding tissue, creating a contrast that drew the eye with biological authority. The nipples themselves protruded permanently now. Not a response to cold or stimulation, a constant state. Each one stood raised from the center of its areola, a small but unmistakable nub that was visible from the side, that would be visible through a thin shirt, that was visible right now in the gray predawn light as a topographic feature of a chest that was losing its male geometry.

And behind the nipples, the tissue. The breast buds that Mira had palpated last night, that she'd described as five or six millimeters, that she'd felt beneath her fingertip as small warm domes. They were, larger this morning. I pressed my palm flat against my left pectoral and moved it slowly downward, from the hard muscle at the top to the yielding softness at the bottom. The transition was gradual but unmistakable. Hard became less hard became soft, and the soft was thicker than yesterday, the layer of new tissue measurably deeper, the dome behind the nipple more pronounced. I cupped my hand experimentally, the way Mira had described, a cupped hand detecting the mounding. My palm curved over the lower edge of the pectoral and, there. The tissue gathered against my hand. Not much. Not a breast. Not anything that anyone would look at and call a breast. But a mounding. A convexity. The first three-dimensional expression of what the nipple had been promising.

My right hand crossed to the other side. Same. Symmetrical. The compound's relentless bilateral precision, building both sides simultaneously, the architecture matching to the millimeter, the blueprints executed with a fidelity that would have been admirable if it weren't being executed on me.

I sat up in the bedroll. The movement shifted the tissue, and I felt it. A weight. Minute, negligible, the mass of a few grams of newly grown glandular tissue responding to gravity as I changed position. The tissue shifted downward by a fraction when I sat upright, and the shift produced a sensation, a gentle pull, a tug at the nipple, the weight of the bud settling into its new orientation. The sensation was slight. It was also completely new. Male chests didn't shift when you sat up. Male chests didn't pull at the nipples with the gravity of new growth. This was the first whisper of a phenomenon that would become louder, much louder, as the mass increased and the tissue expanded and the architecture progressed from bud to breast.

My waist. I pressed my hands against my sides, fingers splayed, measuring the circumference with the crude instrument of my own reach. Narrower. Again. The belt was on its last notch and the last notch was loose, the leather cinching around a waist that had shed another fraction of an inch overnight, the obliques thinning, the flanks trimming, the torso tapering from ribcage to hip in a ratio that was moving away from the rectangular male silhouette and toward something, curved. The word was accurate and unwelcome. My torso was developing a curve. An inward sweep at the waist that created a visual narrowing between the ribcage and the hips, and the visual narrowing was real narrowing, the muscles and subcutaneous fat and even the connective tissue rearranging themselves according to the compound's design.

My hips. I pressed my hands lower, to the iliac crests, the bony ridges at the top of the pelvis that I could feel through the skin. They ached. The deep, structural ache that I'd described to Mira yesterday, not muscle pain, not bruise, not injury. Bone. The bone itself was aching, the pelvis responding to the compound's instructions with the slow, grinding compliance of a structure that had spent thirty-two years in one configuration and was being told to adopt another. The widening had progressed. I couldn't see it, not yet, not through clothing, not even naked unless I measured, but I could feel it. The iliac crests were fractionally further apart this morning than they'd been last night. Millimeters. A distance measurable only by the ache that accompanied it and the subtle change in how my legs hung from my hips when I sat.

I pulled the blanket back up. That was enough inventory.

The fourth morning. The compound was entering its second phase, the changes accelerating, the early subtleties giving way to alterations that were increasingly difficult to ignore, to conceal, to explain. Nella's timeline had called the fourth day the "palpable threshold", the day when the breast tissue became detectable by touch rather than merely by the owner's heightened awareness. The day when the skin lightened noticeably. The day when the voice began its preliminary softening.

I hadn't spoken yet this morning. I was afraid to.

The gray was brightening. Dawn was coming, the light strengthening from suggestion to statement, the forest canopy beginning to resolve from black mass into individual trees and branches and leaves. Somewhere nearby, a thrush began its morning recitation, the same cascading song it had been singing since before humans existed, unchanged and unchanging, the one constant in a world where everything else appeared to be negotiable.

[[Stream|day_7_2]]


:: day_7_2

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The stream was thirty paces from camp, a narrow run of clear water over smooth stones that caught the first real light of morning and threw it back in shattered fragments. I'd heard it all night, the constant murmur, water explaining itself to stone, the sound that had become the soundtrack of my mornings since Valdmere. Every camp had a stream and every stream had become a mirror and every mirror had become a reckoning.

I knelt at the bank. The water was cold, mountain-fed, carrying the chill of snowmelt from the higher elevations we were approaching. I cupped it in my hands and brought it to my face. The shock of cold against skin that was more sensitive than it should have been, the water running over the smooth jaw and the fuller lips and the porcelain surface that four days ago had been roughened by stubble and weathered by sun and scarred by thirty-two years of being a man's face.

The water dripped from my chin. I caught my reflection in the pool beneath the current, distorted, wavering, but real enough. The face that looked back at me was...

It was my face. Still mine. The green eyes, the scar through the left eyebrow, the general architecture of bone and flesh that I'd been wearing since birth. But the rendering had changed. The same features drawn by a different hand, a hand that favored curves over angles, softness over hardness, grace over blunt utility. The jaw that had been square was rounding. The cheekbones that had been buried were emerging. The lips that had been thin and utilitarian were now a feature, full, pigmented, shaped. The skin that had been rough and tanned and lived-in was becoming something almost porcelain, a word I'd never applied to myself and that applied itself now with uncomfortable accuracy.

I was, pretty. The word surfaced unbidden and I drowned it in another handful of cold water.

I stripped off my tunic. The morning air was cold on my bare skin and the nipples responded with their predictable, enhanced contraction, the nubs tightening, the areolae crinkling, the tissue behind them pulling taut against the pectoral muscle. I looked down at my torso in the strengthening light.

The changes were more visible than they'd been in the gray of the bedroll. In the clear light of morning, with the skin wet from the stream, the transformation was, readable. The smooth, hairless chest. The pale, luminous skin. The nipples, raised and pink and permanently protruding from their expanded areolae. The narrowed waist. The faint, gentle convexity behind each nipple, the breast buds, visible now not just to touch but to sight, slight mounds that disrupted the flat plane of the male chest with the first topographic evidence of what was coming.

I looked at my arms. The hair was nearly gone, the dark, thick arm hair that had covered my forearms since adolescence reduced to a fine, almost invisible fuzz. The skin beneath was smooth, the muscle still defined but the definition different, the veins less prominent, the tendons less visible, the overall impression of the forearm shifting from functional tool to something more aesthetically arranged. My hands at the ends of those arms, slimmer. The fingers longer in appearance because the knuckles were less pronounced, the skin over them smoother, the nails growing faster and the nail beds wider and more oval than the squared-off shapes I'd always had.

I washed. Methodically. The way I'd washed at every stream for thirty-two years, face, neck, chest, underarms, arms. The routine unchanged even as the body it serviced was being redesigned. The cold water ran over muscle and new tissue alike, treating them with the same democratic indifference, not caring that the chest it flowed over was caught between two states of being.

I ran my wet hands over my stomach. The abdominal muscles were still there, the compound hadn't touched them yet, the hard ridges of the rectus abdominis still defined, still functional, still the core stability that kept a swordsman upright and a fighter grounded. But the skin over them was different. Smoother. Softer. The trail of hair that had once run from navel to groin, the linea alba, the line that Mira's mouth had followed last night in the ruins, was nearly invisible now. Just the faintest ghost of fine hair, pale against skin that was itself paling, the dark body hair retreating like a tide going out and leaving behind a beach of skin so smooth it caught the light like polished marble.

My hands went lower. To the waistband. Below.

Still there. Still mine. The cock, the balls, the apparatus of maleness that the compound had, so far, left alone. Nella's notes had been specific about this: the genitals were the last territory, the final redoubt, the stronghold that the compound circled and besieged but didn't breach until the later days. The external changes would accelerate, breast growth, hip widening, fat redistribution, facial feminization, while the genitals maintained their function, their size, their essential maleness. A deliberate cruelty in the compound's design, or perhaps a biological necessity, the reproductive organs required more time, more preparation, more comprehensive hormonal restructuring before the compound could begin its final work.

But the surrounding territory was changing. The pubic hair was thinning, the thick, dark tangle reduced to a softer, sparser growth. The skin of the inner thighs was smoother, more sensitive, the nerve density increasing in areas that had previously been functional rather than erogenous. And the space between, the perineum, the tissue behind the scrotum, was more sensitive than I wanted to acknowledge, the compound wiring new pathways through territory that male anatomy barely registered and that the compound was preparing for, something. A future use. A reconfiguration that I understood intellectually from Nella's notes and refused to contemplate emotionally.

I splashed water on my face one more time. Cold. Clarifying. The shock of temperature against skin that felt everything now, that registered the individual droplets as distinct impacts, that experienced the run of water over the jaw and throat as a sensory event rather than a hygiene routine.

I pulled the tunic back on. Laced it up. The fabric lay against my chest and the nipples registered the contact, the rough linen pressing against the swollen, sensitized tissue with a friction that I had to consciously choose to ignore. Every. Single. Time. I moved. The tunic shifted, the fabric dragged across the nipples, and the nipples sent their report upward, contact, pressure, stimulation, and the report arrived in my brain tagged with a significance that tunic-against-chest had never carried before.

I layered the leather vest over the tunic. Buckled it tight across the front. The compression flattened the small mounds, pressing the breast buds against the pectoral muscle, reducing their profile to something that could pass for a muscled male chest under casual observation. The vest was my armor now, not against swords or arrows but against the visual evidence of what was growing underneath it.

I walked back to camp.

[[Mira's Assessment|day_7_3]]


:: day_7_3

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

Mira was awake. Of course she was. The half-elf had a relationship with sleep that bordered on the transactional, she extracted what she needed and released the rest with an efficiency that made human sleep patterns look indulgent. She was crouched by the fire, feeding it small sticks, the flames licking upward in the gray-gold light with the obedient responsiveness of a fire that knew its keeper.

She looked up when I approached. The amber eyes tracked me from the treeline to the fire with the systematic assessment that had become her morning ritual, scanning, cataloging, measuring the overnight changes with the precision of a surveyor documenting erosion. I watched her watch me. Watched the eyes move from my face to my hair to my shoulders to the vest-compressed chest to the waist to the hips and back to the face. The circuit took three seconds. The data was processed in one.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Sit.
<</dialog>>

I sat. On the log she'd positioned by the fire, the same log she positioned every evening, at the same angle, in the same spot relative to the flames, the campcraft so habitual it had become invisible.

She moved to me. Knelt in front of me. Her hands came up and cupped my jaw, turning my face toward the light the way she'd done every morning since the first morning. The same clinical grip, the same evaluative tilt, the same deliberate rotation from profile to profile. But this morning, her hands paused. Her thumbs rested on my cheekbones and her fingers traced the jawline and she held the position longer than usual. Longer than data collection required.

<<dialog "Mira">>
The jaw is softer. Measurably. Yesterday I could feel the angle when I traced the line from the ear, a definite corner, even if the corner was rounding. This morning the corner is, it's becoming a curve, Aldric. Not a corner with a radius. A curve.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I felt it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your cheekbones are more prominent. The soft tissue over them is thinner. I can feel the bone more clearly. And the skin is, you need to see this.
<</dialog>>

She pulled me by the jaw toward the firelight, angling my face so the warm glow caught it directly.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your skin is lighter. Not pale. Not sickly. But the tan is fading. The weathering, the sun damage, the roughness, the texture that comes from years outdoors, it's being replaced. The skin is smoother and lighter and it has a quality that I can only describe as, luminous. It catches the light differently. It reflects more. It glows.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Porcelain. The word I thought of.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Yes. That's, yes. Porcelain. The compound is refinishing you, Aldric. Resurfacing the skin like a craftsman refinishing a table.
<</dialog>>

Her thumb traced my lower lip. I felt it, the touch registering through nerve endings that were denser than they'd been yesterday, each receptor reporting the pressure and the warmth and the specific texture of Mira's thumbprint with excruciating fidelity.

<<dialog "Mira">>
The lips. The cupid's bow is, defined. Yesterday it was forming. Today it's a feature. The lower lip is plush. The color is rosy without any flush from cold or exertion. These are. Aldric, these are a woman's lips. On a face that is still mostly masculine but, less than yesterday. Measurably less.
<</dialog>>

I didn't answer. She wasn't asking me to answer. She was filing her report, delivering the morning briefing with the systematic efficiency that kept the data clean and the emotions contained and the information flowing in the direction it needed to flow, outward, into language, into the shared space between us where clinical observation could serve as a proxy for the fear it described.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Say something.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Why?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Because I need to hear your voice this morning.
<</dialog>>

I understood. She was checking the voice. Nella's timeline had placed the preliminary vocal softening at the fourth day, not a register change, not a pitch shift, but a rounding of the edges, a loss of roughness, the first preparatory adjustments to the vocal cords and the resonant cavities of the throat and chest.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My voice is fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Say more. A full sentence. Something with range.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The crossroads should be four hours' walk from here. We'll reach it by mid-morning if the road is clear and we maintain yesterday's pace. After we part with Hadden, we turn northeast toward Corsham. The mule tracks give us a direction and two days' head start gives Ysolde a lead that we need to close.
<</dialog>>

She listened. Head tilted. The amber eyes narrowed with the focus of a person listening not to the words but to the instrument producing them.

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's softer. Not higher. Not, different in pitch. But the edges are smoother. The consonants are less hard. The sibilants are, rounder. Yesterday your voice had gravel in it. Today the gravel is being ground into sand.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Sand is still rough.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Sand is what gravel becomes on the way to becoming smooth. The trajectory is clear, Aldric. The voice is on the same path as everything else. Different timeline, different pace, but the same destination.
<</dialog>>

A silence. The fire crackled. Hadden's snoring drifted from his bedroll, the metronome, steady and mechanical, the sound of a man sleeping with the uncomplicated efficiency of someone who had no idea that the person sitting twenty paces away was being slowly, systematically transformed into a woman.

<<dialog "Mira">>
How's the chest?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Growing.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I need more than that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The buds are larger. Overnight, again. I can feel the difference from yesterday, more tissue behind each nipple, the mound more defined, more distinct from the surrounding muscle. When I sat up this morning, the tissue shifted. Gravity. The weight is, it's negligible, it's grams, but it moved. It pulled at the nipple when my position changed. That's new. And the nipples themselves are larger, the areolae wider, the color deeper, the protrusion more permanent. The vest hides it. For now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Can I?
<</dialog>>

I unlaced the vest. Then the tunic. The cool morning air touched my chest and the nipples did their thing, the instantaneous contraction, the tightening, the tissue behind them pulling taut. Mira's hands were on me before the lacing was fully undone. Her palms flat against my pectorals, fingers spread, feeling the topology of a chest she'd known for years and was learning anew every morning.

Her hands moved downward. Over the lower edge of the pectoral. Into the new territory, the soft zone, the transitional terrain where hard muscle gave way to yielding tissue, where the architecture shifted from what I had been to what I was becoming. Her palm cupped the lower portion of the left pectoral and the breast bud settled into her hand like a small, warm animal seeking warmth from another source.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Bigger. Definitely bigger than last night. The dome is. I can close my hand slightly and feel it mound against my palm. Not a breast. Not even close. But it's not, it's past the stage where I'd call it invisible. If someone cupped your chest right now, the way I'm doing, they'd feel this. They'd feel something that isn't muscle and isn't fat and has no business being on a man's chest.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Hence the vest.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The vest works. The layering works. Under clothing, with the compression, you're still readable as male. Barely. But. Aldric, we're four days in. Out of thirty. We're at thirteen percent of the timeline and the breast tissue is already palpable. Nella's progression charts had this at the fourth day, which means the compound is on schedule, which means the remaining twenty-six days are going to deliver everything else she predicted, and she predicted —
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know what she predicted.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I need to say it. I need us both to hear it. She predicted D-cup equivalent by day twenty and full size by day twenty-five. She predicted voice change completing by day eight. She predicted hip width reaching female proportions by day twelve. She predicted genital changes beginning at day fifteen and —
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I said I know.
<</dialog>>

My voice came out harder than I intended. Or softer, the hardness was there, the emotion was there, but the instrument delivering it had been sanded down by the compound's overnight work, and the anger that should have come through as a growl arrived as something more strained, more plaintive, the rough edges that used to give my voice its authority removed like barnacles from a hull.

Mira withdrew her hands. Slowly. The way she always did, pulling back with the deliberate care of a person who understood that removing touch was itself a form of communication and that the manner of its withdrawal carried as much meaning as its application.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'll stop.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Don't stop. Don't. I need you to keep doing this. The daily report. The measurements. I need the external perspective because from inside this body, everything is, the subjective experience is unreliable. The sensation floods the data. I need your objectivity because I've lost mine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm not objective.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're closer to it than I am.
<</dialog>>

She looked at me. A long, level look that carried the weight of what she wasn't saying, the things that didn't fit in the clinical report, the observations that belonged in a different category than data, the fact that she was watching her lover transform into a woman and that the watching was complicated in ways that clinical language couldn't contain.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Lace up. Hadden's waking.
<</dialog>>

I laced the tunic. Buckled the vest. Pushed my hair back from my forehead, it fell forward again, the longer strands refusing to obey, the wave in the new growth giving it a movement and a will that my old hair had never possessed. I tucked it behind my ears. It stayed, for the moment. Behind the ears that were. I hadn't checked the ears. Were the ears changing? I filed the question for later. The ears could wait. Everything could wait except the road and the crossroads and the witch at the end of it.

Hadden emerged from his bedroll like a bear from hibernation, blinking, rumpled, his spectacles already on his face because he apparently slept in them, his notebook already in his hand because it was apparently an extension of his arm. He nodded at us. The polite, contained nod that meant good morning and I'm still processing things and my notebook is full of questions I'm not going to ask.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Morning. I see the fire's been tended. Good. I have, notes, from last night. Extensive notes. About the ruins. Do either of you want to hear about Late Imperial document sealing practices? No? Remarkable topic. Another time perhaps.
<</dialog>>

He began packing with the organized chaos that was his signature style, a man who could locate any specific tool in a saddlebag that appeared to contain the entire contents of a small library, arranged by a system that only he understood and that apparently worked flawlessly.

I helped him load Pebble. The mule regarded me with her customary philosophical resignation, the liquid eyes tracking my hands on the straps with the evaluative patience of an animal that had opinions about load distribution and kept them to herself. The straps needed adjusting. My hands, my slimmer, smoother, softer hands, worked the buckles with slightly less certainty than they had two days ago. The calluses that had given me grip on leather and metal were nearly gone, the protective layer dissolved by the compound, leaving fingertips that were more sensitive and less functional, better at feeling and worse at gripping. I compensated. Adjusted my technique. Held the strap with more deliberate pressure to make up for the friction I'd lost.

Hadden watched. The surveyor's eyes, magnified behind his spectacles, noted the adjustment the way they noted everything, without comment, without judgment, with the comprehensive recording instinct that had probably been filing observations about my changing body since the first day and that his professional courtesy prevented him from voicing.

We broke camp. Packed the bedrolls, smothered the fire, cleared the signs of our presence with the efficient routine of people who'd been traveling together long enough that the choreography required no direction. Mira led. I followed. Hadden and Pebble brought up the rear.

The road awaited. The crossroads. The parting. And beyond the parting, the northeast road, the mountains, the witch.

[[The Road to the Crossroads|day_7_4]]


:: day_7_4

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

The morning was gold and green and warm and the road unspooled before us like a sentence being written by the landscape. The Ashenmoor Road here was well-maintained, packed earth, flanked by drainage ditches, wide enough for two carts to pass. Traffic was light. A farmer's wagon heading south, loaded with autumn squash. A pair of riders in the livery of some minor lord, cantering past without a second glance. A walking merchant with a pack twice his size, hunched under the weight like a man carrying his own house, which in a sense he was.

Nobody looked at me twice. The leather armor, the sword, the broad shoulders, the visual shorthand of masculinity that the world read at a distance and filed under "armed man, not my concern." The changes were happening underneath. Beneath the vest, beneath the tunic, beneath the skin. The exterior was still passing, the costume still holding, the performance still convincing enough for a casual audience.

But I felt it. Every step.

The hips. The ache had shifted overnight from a deep, intermittent throb to a constant, low-grade complaint, the bone remodeling with the patient determination of a structure that had been given new instructions and was executing them regardless of the tenant's objections. The widening was progressing. Not dramatically. I wasn't suddenly walking with a woman's pelvis. But the angle was changing. The femoral angle, the way the thighbones connected to the hip socket, was shifting by fractions of a degree, and those fractions accumulated into a gait change that I was increasingly struggling to suppress.

The sway. Mira had mentioned it yesterday and I'd been conscious of it since. The wider pelvis created a rotational component in the walking motion, the hips tilting with each step, right and left, a lateral movement that was the biomechanical consequence of the changing skeletal geometry. In a woman, this produced the natural feminine gait, the hip sway that was as much structural as it was stylistic, the body expressing its architecture through movement. In me, in this transitional state, in this body that was neither fully male nor anywhere close to female, the sway was an intruder. A preview. A physical forecast of a future I was trying to outrun.

I suppressed it. Deliberately. Consciously engaging the gluteal muscles to lock the pelvis, forcing a stride that was mechanical and straight and masculine and utterly exhausting to maintain. Every step was a negotiation. The body wanted to sway and the mind demanded rigidity and the compromise was a gait that felt like walking through water, heavy, deliberate, effortful. I could do it. I could maintain the suppression. But the energy it cost was significant, and the question I didn't want to ask was how many more days I could sustain it before the structural changes outpaced my ability to compensate.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're doing it again.
<</dialog>>

She fell back from the lead to walk beside me, matching my pace, her stride fluid and effortless beside my controlled march.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Doing what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Walking like a soldier. Stiff. Controlled. You're suppressing the natural gait.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm walking normally.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're walking like a man who is trying very hard to walk like a man. There's a difference. The effort is visible. To me, at least.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Would you prefer I sway?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'd prefer you walk in whatever way doesn't exhaust you. Save the performance for audiences that matter. Out here, on an empty road, with Hadden behind us who has already noticed something and isn't asking, out here, you can walk however your body wants to walk.
<</dialog>>

I considered it. The prospect of releasing the conscious suppression, of letting the pelvis do what the pelvis was designed, was being redesigned, to do. Of walking with the sway instead of against it. The temptation was purely physical, the promise of reduced effort, of a stride that flowed rather than fought, of muscles that worked with the new architecture instead of against it.

I released the suppression. Gradually. Let the gluteal lock ease. Let the pelvis find its own rhythm.

The change was immediate. The stride lengthened. The hips tilted, right, left, right, a gentle oscillation that smoothed the walking motion into something more fluid, more efficient, more, natural. The effort dropped. The heavy, water-walking sensation evaporated and in its place was a gait that felt, easy. Comfortable. Correct, in the mechanical sense. The body moving the way the body was now built to move, the biomechanics aligned instead of opposed.

It also felt, feminine. I could feel the sway from the inside. The lateral movement of the hips, the rotational component in each step, the way my thighs brushed past each other in a different pattern than they had a week ago. The gait of a woman. Emerging from the skeleton of a man. Written into the bones by the compound's patient, relentless hand.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Better?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Physically, yes. Better.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
But?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
But I can feel it. The sway. It's, real. It's not a choice or an affectation or a habit. It's structural. The bones have changed enough that the natural gait is different and the different gait is, female. Obviously and specifically female. And if I walk like this in front of anyone who knows me —
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nobody who knows you is on this road. The people who know you are in Valdmere, a week behind us. The people ahead of us. Corsham, Thornfield, have never seen you. They'll see a man in armor with a feminine walk and they'll think old injury. Bad hip. Childhood illness. People explain away what they're not expecting to see.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You didn't explain it away.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know what's happening to you. Context changes perception. Without context, a stranger sees a man who walks oddly. With context, an observer sees a body that's being rewritten.
<</dialog>>

She was right. She was usually right. The practical intelligence that made her a devastating archer and an even more devastating tactician applied to the logistics of concealment with the same efficiency it applied to everything else, identify the problem, assess the resources, deploy the solution, move on.

Behind us, Hadden was talking to Pebble. The ongoing monologue, a one-sided conversation conducted in the gentle, continuous patter of a man who treated his mule as a confidante and whose confidante had the grace to listen without judgment. Pebble's hooves clattered on the packed road in their patient rhythm. The cart, no, the saddlebags, shifted with each step. The morning stretched ahead of us, gold and green and warm.

I let the sway stay. Let the hips do what the hips wanted to do. Let the body walk with its new gait, the feminine rhythm that the compound had built into the bones, the stride that was, easier. Genuinely easier. The energy cost of walking dropped by half when I stopped fighting the mechanics. The body knew what it was doing, even if the mind didn't. The body had been revised and the revision included a new operating manual and the manual specified a gait that was, what it was.

One more concession. One more piece of the old self quietly retired, its replacement installed overnight without consent, the new version smoother and more efficient and utterly, terrifyingly different.

[[The Crossroads|day_7_5]]


:: day_7_5

<<nobr>>
<<set $mcLocation = "corsham_cross">>
<<set $inventory.push({ icon: "🌿", name: "Hadden's Herbal Tea", count: 1, category: "consumable", desc: "Hadden's own blend — chamomile, valerian, elfroot. Two pinches in hot water before sleep. Soothes aching joints." })>>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

We reached the crossroads by mid-morning.

It was a junction, unremarkable in geography, significant in meaning. The Ashenmoor Road continued east, a broad, well-traveled track heading toward the Consortium patrol zones and the civilization that Hadden was returning to. A narrower road branched northeast, climbing gradually toward Corsham and the foothills beyond, the road that would take us toward the mountains, toward Thornfield, toward a witch with a mule and the answers that my body was asking for louder every day.

A stone marker stood at the junction. Old, weathered, the carved letters worn by decades of rain and wind. ASHENMOOR EAST. CORSHAM NORTH-EAST. The simplicity of it, two directions, two futures, the geography imposing a choice that had already been made.

Hadden stopped. Pebble stopped. The mule regarded the crossroads with the same philosophical equanimity she applied to everything, two roads, the same number of legs, the destination a matter of indifference to a creature whose concerns were food, rest, and the reasonable distribution of weight across her spine.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Well. This is where our paths diverge.
<</dialog>>

He said it with the measured formality of a man who had prepared the sentence in advance, composed it in his notebook, probably, refined the phrasing, considered and rejected alternatives. He stood at the junction with his pack squared on his shoulders and his notebook under his arm and his spectacles catching the mid-morning sun and reflecting it back as twin discs of gold.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The Consortium patrol zone. How far?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Two days east. A Consortium waypoint at Millhaven, then the garrison at Locksworth. Standard recovery protocol, file the site report, requisition a recovery team, await escort back to the ruins to supervise the crate extraction. Should be, orderly. Bureaucratic. Deeply satisfying in a institutional-procedure sort of way.
<</dialog>>

He shifted his weight. His eyes came to me and stayed, the evaluative squint, the tilted head, the look of a man who was seeing something he couldn't quite categorize and whose professional instinct wouldn't let him leave the observation unfiled.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Aldric. I want to say something and I want you to know that I've thought about whether to say it and decided that the discomfort of saying it is preferable to the dishonesty of not saying it.
<</dialog>>

I tensed. Mira, beside me, shifted her weight onto the balls of her feet, the archer's readiness, the unconscious preparation for action that her body executed whenever a situation moved toward uncertain territory.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Go ahead.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
You look different than you did two days ago. I noticed it yesterday at the ruins and I notice it more today. Your face is, softer. Your skin is, different. Your walk this morning was different from your walk yesterday, and yesterday's was different from the day before. I am a surveyor. I measure things. I document changes. I have been, not measuring you, not intentionally, but I have been, observing. And the observations are, consistent. Something is changing. About you. About your body. I don't know what it is and I don't need to know what it is and I am not asking you to tell me what it is. I'm saying that I see it. And that whatever it is, whatever is happening. I wish you well with it. Genuinely.
<</dialog>>

The silence that followed was shaped like a held breath. The crossroads held it. The stone marker held it. The morning held it.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's a medical condition. Alchemical. We're seeking treatment in Thornfield. That's, that's all I can say.
<</dialog>>

Hadden nodded. The slow, careful nod of a man accepting a partial truth and choosing to treat it as sufficient.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Alchemical. Yes. That would explain the rate of change. Alchemical processes operate on accelerated timelines, the body forced to adapt at speeds that natural biology doesn't accommodate. I've read about such things. Mostly theoretical. Mostly in texts that the academic community considers dubious. But the evidence is, well. The evidence is standing in front of me with a softer jawline and fuller lips and a gait that has changed twice in two days. The evidence is compelling.
<</dialog>>

He stepped forward. Extended his hand. I took it, the handshake, the simple, universal gesture of parting. His grip was firm, dry, professional. My hand in his was, different. He felt it. I watched his eyes register the change, the softer skin, the slimmer fingers, the absence of the calluses that had roughened my grip two days ago. He didn't comment. He held the handshake for a beat longer than convention required and then released.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Mira.
<</dialog>>

She shook his hand. Brief, professional, the half-elf efficiency.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
I've written the completion letter for Voss.
<</dialog>>

He produced a sealed envelope from his coat, properly sealed, wax stamp, the Consortium insignia pressed into the red wax with the care of a man who took official correspondence seriously.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Your payment will be waiting in Valdmere when you return. Two gold, as agreed. The letter attests to exemplary service, beyond expectations, in fact. The ruins would still be buried without your labor, Aldric. The crates would still be sealed. My report to the Consortium will reflect the quality of the escort and the, reliability of the team.
<</dialog>>

Mira took the letter. Tucked it into her pack with the efficient precision of a woman who filed important documents the way she stored arrowheads, categorized, accessible, protected.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
One more thing.
<</dialog>>

He reached into his saddlebag, the capacious, book-stuffed saddlebag that served as his mobile office, and produced a small leather pouch. He pressed it into my hand.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
A blend. Herbal. My own formulation, chamomile, valerian, a touch of elfroot for the joints. I noticed you favoring your hips yesterday. Growing pains, perhaps, or the alchemical condition, or just the road. Whatever the cause, this tea helps. Two pinches in hot water before sleep. It won't cure anything, but it soothes the ache. I've used it for my own joints for years and the relief is, meaningful.
<</dialog>>

I looked at the pouch. Small, practical, thoughtful. A gift from a man who noticed things and cared about things and expressed both through the medium of organized kindness.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Thank you, Hadden.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Safe travels. Both of you. Find your healer. Get your treatment. And if you're ever in Valdmere and want to hear about Late Imperial document sealing practices, you know where the Consortium office is.
<</dialog>>

He turned. Pebble turned with him, the mule pivoting on her hooves with the practiced grace of an animal that understood directional changes and executed them without fuss. They walked east. The Ashenmoor Road received them, the broad, maintained track absorbing the stocky figure and the patient mule into its length, the distance between us growing with each step, the gap widening the way gaps do when two trajectories diverge from a shared point.

I watched him go. The notebook was already out. The charcoal stick was already moving. Hadden, documenting his departure as he made it, writing the story of the road even as the road unfolded beneath his feet.

<<dialog "Mira">>
He's a good man.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
He noticed everything.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He noticed everything and said nothing until the end and what he said at the end was kind. That's, rarer than you think.
<</dialog>>

She was right. She was always right about people. The half-elf read humans the way she read terrain, systematically, accurately, without sentiment.

I looked northeast. The narrower road climbed into the trees, the packed earth giving way to gravel as the elevation increased, the canopy closing overhead. The road to Corsham. The road to Thornfield. The road to Ysolde.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Let's move.
<</dialog>>

We turned northeast. The crossroads fell behind us. Hadden and Pebble disappeared around a bend in the eastern road, the surveyor and his mule swallowed by distance and the green patience of the forest.

The narrower road received us. We walked into it, two figures, one armed with a bow and the other with a sword, heading northeast into the hills with a sealed letter and a pouch of herbal tea and the knowledge that time was a resource we were spending faster than we could earn.

My hips swayed as I walked. I let them.

[[The Northeast Road|day_7_6]]


:: day_7_6

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/4.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The northeast road was a different country.

The Ashenmoor Road had been civilization's claim on the landscape, broad, maintained, trafficked, the kind of road that implied tax collection and military patrols and the organized movement of goods and people. The northeast road was civilization's apology. Narrower by half, gravel replacing packed earth, the drainage ditches shallow and intermittent, the canopy closing overhead to create a green tunnel that the sun penetrated in shafts and columns rather than blankets.

The forest changed with the road. The broad oaks and beeches of the lowlands gave way to pine and spruce as we gained elevation, taller, straighter, darker trees, their trunks rising like columns in a cathedral of wood, the canopy high and dense and fragrant with resin. The undergrowth thickened, fern and bracken and the creeping ground cover that thrived in the filtered light, green things reaching for the sun through the cracks in the canopy with the patient determination of life that would not be denied.

The air changed. Cooler. Thinner. Each breath carried the mineral edge of higher elevation, the scent of stone and snowmelt mixing with the pine resin and the damp earth. We were climbing. Not steeply, the road was graded for carts and mules and merchants who valued their knees, but steadily, the elevation gaining with each mile, the landscape tilting upward toward the foothills that we could see in the distance when the canopy broke, gray-green ridges stacked against the sky like pages in a book.

The traffic thinned. We passed a tinker heading south, a man whose cart was a mobile hardware store, pots and pans and tools and implements hanging from every surface, the whole apparatus chiming and clanking like a one-man orchestra of domestic utility. He nodded at us. We nodded back. His eyes slid over me without interest, armed man, not a customer, not worth the sales pitch. The leather armor and the sword were doing their work.

A pair of monks in brown robes, shuffling along the road with the unhurried pace of men whose destination was spiritual rather than geographical. They carried nothing but their robes and their faith and a small sack that probably contained bread and a few dried apples. They bowed their tonsured heads as they passed. Mira returned the bow. I didn't. My attention was on their eyes, whether they lingered, whether they saw something in my face that the tinker hadn't caught, whether the closer inspection of a walking encounter revealed what the glancing assessment of a passing one concealed.

They didn't linger. They saw a man. Or close enough. The armor, the posture, the presence of the sword, the visual shorthand of masculinity read at a speed that outpaced the subtler signals of a softening jaw and fuller lips and skin that was slowly losing its tan.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Stop it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Stop what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Stop scanning everyone who passes for signs that they've noticed. You're creating the suspicion you're trying to detect. A man who watches a passing stranger's eyes that carefully is a man who expects to be seen, and the expectation itself draws attention.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What if someone —
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nobody. Has. Noticed. The tinker was pricing our equipment. The monks were praying. The farmer we passed an hour ago was managing goats. People are consumed by their own concerns, Aldric. You are not the center of every stranger's attention. You are a man in armor on a road, and the transformation that feels catastrophic from inside your skin is invisible at ten paces under clothing and in motion.
<</dialog>>

She was right. Again. The compound was changing my body but my paranoia was doing its own renovation, constructing a surveillance architecture in my mind, installing observation posts at every interaction, assigning watchers to every passing face. The fear of being seen was becoming a more constant companion than the changes themselves.

We walked. The road climbed. The morning deepened into early afternoon, the sun tracking westward across the canopy gap, the shadows shortening and then beginning their afternoon extension. We ate as we walked, dried meat, hard bread, an apple each from the supply bag. The food was mechanical. The body required fuel and the fuel was provided and the transaction was completed without pleasure or ceremony.

My body was louder today. Every step brought sensation. The hips ached and the ache was constant now, a bass note beneath the walking rhythm, the deep structural complaint of bone being reshaped. The vest compressed my chest and the nipples registered the compression with every breath, the swollen tissue pressed against the linen and leather, the friction producing a low-grade stimulation that hovered at the border between discomfort and something else. My thighs brushed past each other differently, the inner surfaces smoother, the muscle contours subtly altered, the legs hanging from the widening pelvis at angles that created new contact patterns with every stride.

And beneath all of it, the warmth. The compound's warmth, the metabolic heat of transformation, the thermal signature of a body burning calories to rebuild itself. The warmth was concentrated in the chest today, the breast buds radiating a gentle, persistent heat that I could feel through the tunic, a reminder that the tissue was active, was growing, was consuming resources and producing results with the compound's relentless, patient efficiency.

I was sweating. The exertion of the climb, the warmth of the compound, the layered clothing that concealment required, the combination produced a sheen of perspiration that gathered at my hairline and ran down my temples and collected in the hollow of my throat. The sweat was different. Not the sharp, acrid sweat of a working man, the salt-heavy, testosterone-laden perspiration that I'd produced for thirty-two years. This was lighter. Cleaner. The compound was changing even my sweat, altering the chemistry of the apocrine glands, reducing the androgen-driven components that gave male sweat its distinctive character. I smelled, different. Less. The body odor that had been my constant companion, the musky baseline that I'd never noticed until it began to change, was fading. Being replaced by something milder, sweeter, the biochemistry of a body that was running on a different hormonal cocktail.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Water break. You're flushed.
<</dialog>>

We stopped. I drank from the waterskin, long, deep pulls, the cold water running down my throat, which was less thick than it had been a week ago, the Adam's apple unchanged but the surrounding tissue thinning, the neck slimming toward a profile that was, well. Another item on the list.

Mira watched me drink. Her amber eyes tracked the movement of my throat, the water running down, the skin of my neck catching the filtered sunlight.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your neck.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What about my neck.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's thinner. The muscle at the sides, the sternocleidomastoid, it's less prominent. The neck is beginning to look, longer. Or rather, the same length but with less mass. The tendons are more visible. The skin sits closer to the structure beneath. And the Adam's apple is still there but it's less, pronounced. Not smaller, I don't think. But the neck around it is thinner, so it appears more isolated. More visible because there's less competing anatomy around it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Nella's notes said the Adam's apple itself doesn't change until the eighth day.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm not saying it's changed. I'm saying the context around it has changed, which changes how it reads. A landmark looks different when you clear the trees around it.
<</dialog>>

I put the waterskin away. We walked.

The afternoon stretched ahead of us, the road climbing, the forest pressing close, the air cooling with each hundred feet of elevation gained. We were alone on the road now, no tinkers, no monks, no farmers. Just the two of us and the pine-scented silence and the sound of gravel under boots and the steady, rhythmic sway of my hips as I walked with the gait that the compound had built and that I'd stopped fighting.

[[The Village|day_7_7]]


:: day_7_7

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The village appeared around a bend in the road, if village was even the right word. A cluster of houses, six, maybe seven, arranged around a well with the haphazard geometry of structures that had been placed by convenience rather than plan. A small trading post that was really just a wooden shack with a counter and a awning. A stable that housed more opinion than horse. And a notice board, the universal communication infrastructure of rural Kothwin, the wooden rectangle covered in nailed parchments and pinned notices that served as newspaper, job board, warning system, and community bulletin all at once.

We attracted looks. Not suspicious looks, curious looks, the kind that small communities directed at anyone who wasn't from there, the reflexive assessment of strangers that was part vigilance and part entertainment in places where entertainment was in short supply. A woman hanging laundry paused to watch us pass. An old man sitting on a bench with a pipe and a dog and the accumulated patience of decades tracked us with eyes that missed nothing and cared about less. Two children stopped their game, some variation of tag that involved a stick and a lot of shrieking, and stared with the undisguised fascination that children applied to anything unfamiliar.

None of them saw anything wrong. I was a man in armor. Mira was a woman with a bow. We were travelers on the northeast road, heading toward Corsham or beyond, armed and competent and not their problem.

Mira went to the notice board. Habit. The archer's practice of collecting intelligence from every available source, however mundane, you never knew which piece of information would turn from trivial to critical, which notice posted in a village that barely existed would redirect the path of the chase.

I waited by the well. Drew water. The bucket came up heavy and cold and I drank from the ladle, the water tasting of mineral and iron and depth, drawn from some subterranean source that had never seen sunlight and didn't care about the concerns of the sunlit world above it.

The woman hanging laundry smiled at me. I returned the smile, the social reflex, the automatic, polite response. And caught myself. My smile felt different. The lips that curved were fuller, the expression they created softer, the smile itself reading differently because the face producing it was different. Did she see a man smiling? Or did she see something more ambiguous, a face that was hovering between categories, a smile that carried signals from two genders at once?

She went back to her laundry. The smile hadn't alarmed her. Whatever she'd seen, it hadn't triggered the uncanny-valley response that I feared, the cognitive dissonance of a face that didn't match its context, a woman's features on a man's frame, the visual contradiction that would make strangers stare and then look away and then stare again.

Mira returned from the notice board. Her expression was the one she wore when she'd found something, the controlled intensity, the amber eyes carrying new data.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Look at this.
<</dialog>>

She led me to the board. Pointed at a notice pinned near the center, newer paper, dark ink, the handwriting of someone who'd been trained to write clearly.

<div style="border: 1px solid var(--clr-border, #4a3a2a); padding: 0.8em 1.2em; margin: 1em auto; max-width: 480px; background: rgba(20,14,8,0.5); font-style: italic; color: var(--clr-text, #c8b89a);">
WANTED: Able-bodied fighters for escort work. Merchant convoy departing Corsham for Thornfield, 3 days. 10 silver per fighter. Inquire at the Corsham Garrison.
</div>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Thornfield.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Thornfield. Where Nella's notes said Ysolde might be heading. Where the mule tracks we found yesterday point toward. Where the anti-compound is.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
A convoy. That's our ride.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If we make Corsham in time. The notice says three days, which might mean three days from when it was posted or three days from now. Either way, we need to move.
<</dialog>>

I studied the notice. Ten silver. Thornfield. Three days.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How far to Corsham from here?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The board says two days at merchant pace. We're faster than merchants. Hard push, we could make it in a day and a half. Arrive tomorrow evening.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
If the convoy hasn't left.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If the convoy hasn't left. But merchant convoys are slow to organize. They wait for a full complement of escorts. They wait for the cargo to be loaded. They wait because waiting is what merchants do before they commit money to road. Three days is optimistic by merchant standards. We have time.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
One problem. The convoy requires able-bodied fighters. They'll inspect the applicants. They'll want to see competence. If someone looks too closely —
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You pass. Under armor, in motion, you pass. The face is different but not, not impossibly different. A man with soft features. A man who takes care of his skin. A man who, looks young. That's what Hadden said. Younger. People will see what they expect to see, and what they expect to see when they look at a person in armor with a sword is a man.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
For how much longer?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Long enough. We need it to hold for, what, a week? Get to Corsham. Join the convoy. Reach Thornfield. Find Ysolde. One week. The compound is four days in out of thirty. The changes are progressing but the major transformations, the breast growth, the hip width, the voice, those are still ahead. You have a window. We use it.
<</dialog>>

She said it with the flat conviction of a tactician laying out a plan, not hopeful, not reassuring, but certain. A statement of operational reality. The window existed. The window was closing. The mission was to accomplish the objective before the window sealed shut.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We take the convoy.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We take the convoy.
<</dialog>>

We left the village. The laundry woman waved. The old man's dog barked once and was silenced by a gentle hand on its head. The children had resumed their game, the strangers already filed and forgotten, the brief disruption to the village's routine healed as quickly as it had opened.

The road climbed. The forest deepened. The afternoon light slanted through the pines in golden columns that illuminated the dust our boots raised from the gravel. Behind us, the village settled back into its quiet existence. Ahead, the road led northeast toward Corsham and the convoy and Thornfield and the witch.

My pace was quick. The sway was there, the hip rotation that I'd released, the feminine gait that was the biomechanical consequence of a changing pelvis, but the speed counteracted it, the urgency of the walk overwhelming the subtlety of the motion. We were moving fast now. The notice had given us a deadline and deadlines were fuel.

[[Goblin Tracks|day_7_8]]


:: day_7_8

<<nobr>>
<</nobr>>

<img src="img/scene/day_7/6.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Two hours past the village, Mira stopped.

The stop was instantaneous, the half-elf halt, the complete cessation of motion that her body could achieve in the space between heartbeats. One step she was walking, the next she was frozen, her weight centered, her eyes locked on the ground, her right hand reaching back for the bow with the muscle-memory efficiency of a reflex that had been trained to the point of autonomy.

